<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1319–<a href="/years/1364">1364</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John the Good · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II, called the Good, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1350 to 1364, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. He was captured by the English at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356 and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, while his son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, faced revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the Jacquerie. His long quarrel with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles the Bad</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, included Charles's arrest in 1356.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-france.jpg" alt="John II of France" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_II_Le_Bon_-_BnF_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peinures_RF_2490.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>King John II</b> of France took the duchy as nearest heir and in 1363 granted it to his youngest son, Philip the Bold, as an appanage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hundred Years' War with England (1337–1453) began disastrously: English archers destroyed the French cavalry at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, <b>King John II</b> was captured at the Battle of Poitiers in 1356, and defeat at the Battle of Agincourt followed in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By an ordinance issued at Compiègne, the newly released <b>John II of France</b> created the gold franc, partly to raise his ransom.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-5">5 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Edward III of England and the captive <b>John II of France</b> agreed the Treaty of Brétigny, ending the first phase of the Hundred Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-8">8 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward III and <b>John II of France</b> ratified at Calais the peace agreed at Brétigny in May.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-24">24 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1363 <b>John II of France</b> gave the Duchy of Burgundy to his son Philip the Bold.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48,0.2&amp;year=1348" aria-label="Le Mans on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4555 483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John II of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5005 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5005 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48,0.2&amp;year=1348">What was here in 1348? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
